lead.Ambazonia:
{{Short description|Proclaimed political entity in Cameroon}}
{{distinguish|text=the historical colony of [[Southern Cameroons]], or the [[Amazonia]]n rainforests}}
{{use dmy dates|date=September 2024}}
<!--Do not add a country infobox to this article. See the talk page discussion at [[Talk:Ambazonia#Tagging]] for details.-->

[[File:Flag of the Federal Republic of Southern Cameroons.svg|thumb|The [[flag of Ambazonia]], used by separatists since 1999]]
[[File:Map of the Federal Republic of Ambazonia (claimed).png |thumb |Territory (colored red) within [[Cameroon]] claimed by separatists, corresponding to the country's [[Northwest Region (Cameroon)|Northwest Region]] and [[Southwest Region (Cameroon)|Southwest Region]]]]
'''Ambazonia''', alternatively the '''Federal Republic of Ambazonia''' or the '''State of Ambazonia''',{{sfn|Nkwain|2022|p=245}} is a political entity<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.voaafrica.com/a/cameroon-military-executing-abusing---hrw/6697215.html |title="Cameroon Military Executing, Abusing" - HRW |agency=Agence France-Presse |publisher=Voice of America |date=11 August 2022 |access-date=22 March 2023 |quote=In 2017, resentment at perceived discrimination snowballed into the declaration of an independent state -- the 'Federal Republic of Ambazonia,' an entity that is not recognised internationally.}}</ref> proclaimed by [[Anglophone Cameroonian|Anglophone]] [[Separatism|separatists]] seeking independence from [[Cameroon]]. The separatists claim that Ambazonia should consist of the [[Northwest Region (Cameroon)|Northwest Region]] and [[Southwest Region (Cameroon)|Southwest Region]] of Cameroon. Since 2017, Ambazonian rebels have engaged in armed conflict with the Cameroonian military, in what is known as the [[Anglophone Crisis]], and have attempted to set up [[government-in-exile|governments-in-exile]], and supportive militias have exerted control over parts of the claimed territory. No country has recognized Ambazonia's existence as of 2026.

Until 1961, the territory of these regions was the [[Southern Cameroons|southern part]] of a British trust territory, [[British Cameroon]] while the rest of Cameroon was a French trust territory, [[French Cameroon]]. At independence, [[1961 British Cameroons referendum|a plebiscite]] was held, and voters in Southern Cameroons opted to join Cameroon as a constituent state of [[Federal Republic of Cameroon|a federal republic]].<ref>Nohlen, D, Krennerich, M & Thibaut, B (1999) ''Elections in Africa: A data handbook'', p177 {{ISBN|978-0-19-829645-4}}</ref> Over time, the power of the central government, dominated by [[Francophones]], expanded at the expense of the region's autonomy. Many inhabitants identify as [[Anglophones]] and resent what they perceive as discrimination and efforts to eliminate Anglophone legal, administrative, educational, and cultural institutions by the Cameroonian government.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://theconversation.com/cameroon-how-language-plunged-a-country-into-deadly-conflict-with-no-end-in-sight-179027 |title=Cameroon: how language plunged a country into deadly conflict with no end in sight |author=Rogers Orock |publisher=The Conversation |date=11 August 2022 |access-date=22 March 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theafricareport.com/228634/cameroon-crisis-grinds-on-due-to-anglophone-divisions-and-yaoundes-unwillingness-to-negotiate/ |title=Cameroon: Crisis grinds on due to anglophone divisions, Yaoundé's unwillingness to negotiate |author=Nancy-Wangue Moussissa |publisher=The Africa Report |date=2 August 2022 |access-date=22 March 2023 }}</ref>

In 2016 and 2017, [[2016–17 Cameroonian protests|a widespread protest movement]] was met with a violent government crackdown, which led to rioting and violence against security forces and, in 2017, a unilateral declaration of independence by Ambazonian leaders.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.dw.com/en/who-are-cameroons-self-named-ambazonia-secessionists/a-50639426|title=Who are Cameroon's self-named Ambazonia secessionists? |date=30 September 2019 |publisher=DW |language=en-GB|access-date=2020-01-07}}</ref> The violence developed into a [[guerrilla war]], and {{As of|2023|lc=y}},<ref name="VOA 2023">{{cite web |last1=Kindzeka |first1=Moki |title=Cameroon Separatists Enforce Curfew After President Says Troops Crushing Rebellion |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/cameroon-separatists-enforce-curfew-after-president-says-troops-crushing-rebellion-/6900656.html |website=Voice of America |date=2 January 2023 |publisher=VOA |access-date=3 February 2023}}</ref> clashes continue, with population centers and strategic locations largely controlled by the government engaged in [[counterinsurgency]] actions, with swathes of more remote, rural areas controlled by separatist militias and used to launch guerrilla attacks.<ref name="crisisgroupmay22019">[https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/central-africa/cameroon/272-crise-anglophone-au-cameroun-comment-arriver-aux-pourparlers Cameroon's Anglophone Crisis: How to Get to Talks?], Crisis Group, 2 May 2019. Accessed 2 May 2019.</ref> Ambazonian forces have struggled to form a united front, and internecine conflicts have hampered efforts to negotiate with Cameroon or establish control over the various [[Militia|militia groups]] engaged in the fighting.<ref name="newhumanitarianfeb62020">[https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/news/2020/02/06/Cameroon-elections-anglophone-separatist-insurgency-Ambazonia Briefing: Cameroon's intensifying conflict and what it means for civilians], The New Humanitarian, 6 February 2020. Accessed 18 August 2021.</ref> Ongoing violence has led to widely reported [[human rights]] abuses by both sides, including [[Massacre|indiscriminate killing]] of civilians, [[torture]], [[rape]] and other gender-based crimes, [[False imprisonment|unjustified detentions]], and [[kidnapping]]s.<ref name="auto1">{{cite book |title=HRW World Report 2020: Rights Trends in Cameroon |chapter=Cameroon: Events of 2019 |url=https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2020/country-chapters/cameroon# |date=10 December 2019 |publisher=Human Rights Watch |access-date=August 18, 2021}}</ref>

