The '''Bangladesh Nationalist Party''',{{efn|{{langx|bn|বাংলাদেশ জাতীয়তাবাদী দল|Bāṅlādēś Jātīẏôtābādī Dôl}}, {{IPA|bn|ˈbaŋlaˌdeʃ ˈdʒati̯oˌtabadi dɔl|pron}}}} abbreviated as '''BNP''', is a major [[Centrism|centre]] to [[Centre-right politics|centre-right]] [[political party in Bangladesh]]. It was founded by the former [[President of Bangladesh|president]] [[Ziaur Rahman]] in 1978, adhering the ideology of [[Bangladeshi nationalism]]. It is one of the two contemporary mainstream parties in the country, alongside the [[Bangladesh Jamaat-e-Islami]],<ref name="citation_no1">{{cite web|first=Faisal|last=Mahmud|title=Analysis: Bangladesh's BNP seeks Hasina's liberal mantle before elections|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/9/analysis-bangladeshs-bnp-seeks-hasinas-liberal-mantle-before-elections|website=[[Al Jazeera]]|date=10 December 2025|access-date=13 December 2025|archive-date=11 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251211093243/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/9/analysis-bangladeshs-bnp-seeks-hasinas-liberal-mantle-before-elections|url-status=live}}</ref> and the ruling party since [[2026 Bangladeshi general election|2026]].

It was Known as the "[[Mukti Bahini|Party of the Freedom Fighters of the Battlefield]]" during its establishment,<ref name="dhakatribune">{{cite news |script-title=bn:রাজনৈতিক চরিত্র হারাচ্ছে বিএনপি! |trans-title=BNP is losing political character! |url=https://www.banglatribune.com/politics/79221/রাজনৈতিক-চরিত্র-হারাচ্ছে-বিএনপি|newspaper=[[Bangla Tribune]] |date=18 February 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240815121642/https://www.banglatribune.com/politics/79221/রাজনৈতিক-চরিত্র-হারাচ্ছে-বিএনপি|access-date=21 March 2024 |archive-date=15 August 2024 |language=bn}}</ref> the Bangladesh Nationalist Party was founded on 1 September 1978 by President Ziaur Rahman and remained in his leadership until he was [[Assassination of Ziaur Rahman|assassinated]] in 1981. Following Rahman's assassination, his widow, [[Khaleda Zia]], took over leadership of the party and presided as chairperson until her imprisonment in 2018. From 2018 until 2025, [[Tarique Rahman]], the son of Rahman and Zia, ran the affairs of the party as the acting chairperson. Upon Zia's death in 2025, Rahman became the chairperson of the party.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Hasnat |first1=Saif |last2=Mshal |first2=Mujib |date=2025-12-25 |title=A Top Candidate for Prime Minister Returns to Bangladesh After 17 Years in Exile |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/12/25/world/asia/bangladesh-tarique-rahman-return-exile.html |access-date=2025-12-31 |website=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Bangladesh opposition leader Tarique Rahman returns after 17 years in exile |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/25/bangladesh-opposition-leader-tarique-rahman-returns-after-17-years-in-exile |access-date=25 December 2025 |work=[[Al Jazeera]] |date=25 December 2025 |language=en |archive-date=25 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251225065244/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/25/bangladesh-opposition-leader-tarique-rahman-returns-after-17-years-in-exile |url-status=live }}</ref>

Since its founding, the BNP has won the [[1981 Bangladeshi presidential election|1981]], [[1991 Bangladeshi presidential election|1991]], and [[2001 Bangladeshi presidential election|2001]] presidential elections, and the [[1979 Bangladeshi general election|1979]], [[1991 Bangladeshi general election|1991]], [[February 1996 Bangladeshi general election|February 1996]], [[2001 Bangladeshi general election|2001]], and [[2026 Bangladeshi general election|2026]] general elections.<ref name="IPU91">{{cite web |title=BANGLADESH: parliamentary elections Jatiya Sangsad, 1991 |url=http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/arc/2023_91.htm |access-date=2 January 2019 |publisher=Inter-Parliamentary Union |archive-date=6 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180806213441/http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/arc/2023_91.htm |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Opposition primed to win Bangladesh poll |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1996/06/13/Opposition-primed-to-win-Bangladesh-poll/9522834638400/ |access-date=5 January 2023 |website=UPI |archive-date=3 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250803150047/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1996/06/13/Opposition-primed-to-win-Bangladesh-poll/9522834638400/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Bangladesh parliamentary Elections 1 October 2001: Final Report |url=https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/1412745/625_tmpphpGBRYSw.pdf |access-date=27 December 2018 |website=EU Election Observation Mission|archive-date=8 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221008122854/https://www.ecoi.net/en/file/local/1412745/625_tmpphpGBRYSw.pdf |url-status=live}}</ref> BNP members Ziaur Rahman, [[Abdus Sattar (president)|Abdus Sattar]], [[Abdur Rahman Biswas]], [[A. Q. M. Badruddoza Chowdhury]], and [[Muhammad Jamiruddin Sircar]] have served as the country's president, while [[Mashiur Rahman (politician, born 1924)|Mashiur Rahman]], [[Shah Azizur Rahman]], [[Khaleda Zia]], and [[Tarique Rahman]] have served as the country's prime minister. Ziaur Rahman-led governments formed under the [[semi-presidential system]] and the [[parliamentary republics]] were led by Khaleda Zia, who served as [[Prime minister of Bangladesh|prime minister]].<ref name="citation_no2">{{cite web |title=Begum Khaleda Zia – |url=https://awpc.cattcenter.iastate.edu/directory/begum-khaleda-zia/ |access-date=5 January 2023 |website=Archives of Women's Political Communication |archive-date=5 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105193042/https://awpc.cattcenter.iastate.edu/directory/begum-khaleda-zia/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Khaleda Zia, who served as the party's chairperson from 1983, became the first woman prime minister of Bangladesh and the second female prime minister of a [[Muslim World|Muslim-majority country]] after [[Pakistan]]'s [[Benazir Bhutto]]. The party holds the record of being the largest opposition in the history of parliamentary elections of the country with 116 seats in the [[June 1996 Bangladeshi general election|June 1996 general election]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Report |url=http://www.ecs.gov.bd/MenuExternalFilesEng/125.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130729230304/http://www.ecs.gov.bd/MenuExternalFilesEng/125.pdf |archive-date=29 July 2013 |access-date=13 December 2012}}</ref>

A [[big tent]] party, the BNP espouses a stance that combines a sense of national pride with a commitment to traditional values, while emphasising advancing the interests of corporations. It maintains a solid stance on [[Bangladesh–India relations|relations with India]] while also embracing a blend of moderate Islam and traditional [[Bengali culture]].<ref name="ajis">{{cite journal|first1=Md. Mahdi|last1=Hasan|first2=Bhirawa|last2=Anoraga|date=5 September 2025|title=The Shifting Stands of Secularims: Bangladesh's Response to Religious Conservatism|journal=Australian Journal of Islamic Studies|volume=10, no. 2|issue=2025|pages=90–122|doi=10.55831/ajis.v10i2.733 |doi-access=free}}</ref> The party has faced sustained criticism for [[Corruption in Bangladesh#Bangladesh Nationalist Party|corruption]], political violence, and poor governance, particularly during its [[Third Khaleda ministry|2001–2006 tenure]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sujan |first=Moudud Ahmmed |title=Khaleda Zia, Bangladesh's first woman PM: A life of power and resistance |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2025/12/30/khaleda-zia-bangladeshs-first-woman-pm-a-life-of-power-and-resistance |access-date=2026-01-16 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en |archive-date=5 January 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260105095627/https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2025/12/30/khaleda-zia-bangladeshs-first-woman-pm-a-life-of-power-and-resistance |url-status=live }}</ref> with controversies such as the [[2004 Dhaka grenade attack]], the [[2004 arms and ammunition haul in Chittagong]], and [[Hawa Bhaban]]'s alleged role as a parallel power center.<ref>{{Cite web |title=It was Hawa Bhaban plot |url=https://www.thedailystar.net/news-detail-111469 |access-date=2026-01-16 |website=The Daily Star |language=en |archive-date=17 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117122749/https://www.thedailystar.net/news-detail-111469 |url-status=live }}</ref> The party's affiliated wings have been repeatedly accused of extortion<ref>{{Cite web |title=Local BNP leaders accused of extortion in Netrokona |url=https://www.thedailystar.net/news/local-bnp-leaders-accused-extortion-netrokona-4079361 |access-date=2026-01-16 |website=The Daily Star |language=en}}</ref> and violence, including a [[Killing of Lal Chand Sohag|2025 daylight murder]] linked to its student and youth fronts,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-07-12 |title=Lal Chand was beaten and bludgeoned to death |url=https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/crime-and-law/rasvtbwz4k |access-date=2026-01-16 |website=Prothomalo |language=en |archive-date=19 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250919013658/https://en.prothomalo.com/bangladesh/crime-and-law/rasvtbwz4k |url-status=live }}</ref> further deepening public discontent and damaging the BNP's reputation.<ref>{{Cite news |title=BNP expels four Sitakunda unit leaders over nomination-related violence |url=https://www.newagebd.net/post/politics/281118/bnp-expels-four-sitakunda-unit-leaders-over-nomination-related-violence |work=New Age}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-12-28 |title=ACC sues Cumilla-8 BNP nominee Zakaria, 30 others over Tk647cr loan fraud |url=https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/corruption/acc-sues-cumilla-8-bnp-nominee-zakaria-30-others-over-tk647cr-loan-fraud |access-date=2026-01-16 |website=The Business Standard |language=en |archive-date=28 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251228135927/https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/corruption/acc-sues-cumilla-8-bnp-nominee-zakaria-30-others-over-tk647cr-loan-fraud |url-status=live }}</ref> As of 2026, the Bangladesh Nationalist Party's strongest demographics include working-age voters, farmers, labourers,<ref name="chathamhouse" /> [[Sufism|Sufis]], [[Ahmadiyya in Bangladesh|Ahmadis]],<ref name="netranews" /> [[Hinduism in Bangladesh|Hindus]],<ref name="netranews" /><ref name="thesentinel" /> and [[Ethnic groups in the Chittagong Hill Tracts|indigenous peoples of the Chittagong Hill Tracts]].<ref name="netranews" />

