The '''Estonian Centre Party''' ({{langx|et|Eesti Keskerakond}}, '''KE''') is a [[populist]] political party in [[Estonia]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Santana |first1=Andrés |last2=Zagórski |first2=Piotr |last3=Rama |first3=José |date=2 April 2020 |title=At odds with Europe: explaining populist radical right voting in Central and Eastern Europe |journal=East European Politics |language=en |volume=36 |issue=2 |pages=288–309 |doi=10.1080/21599165.2020.1737523 |s2cid=216476001 |issn=2159-9165|doi-access=free }}</ref> It was founded in 1991 as a direct successor of the [[Popular Front of Estonia]], and it is currently led by [[Mihhail Kõlvart]].<ref>[https://uudised.err.ee/v/eesti/dc2a9297-226d-4789-aa24-7acc192bd33d/keskerakond-valis-esimeheks-juri-ratase], Postimees, 5 November 2016</ref>
The party was founded on 12 October 1991 from the basis of the [[Popular Front of Estonia]] after several parties split from it. At that time, the party was called the People's Centre Party (''Rahvakeskerakond'') to differentiate from the smaller centre-right Rural Centre Party (''Maa-Keskerakond''). The party was a member of the [[Alliance of Liberals and Democrats for Europe Party|Alliance of Liberals and Democrats for Europe]] until 14 September 2024.<ref>{{cite web |title=Eesti Keskerakonna volikogu otsus: lahkume ALDE-st - Keskerakond |url=https://keskerakond.ee/et/meedia/3450-eesti-keskerakonna-volikogu-otsus-lahkume-alde-st.html |website=keskerakond.ee |access-date=14 September 2024}}</ref> On 14 March 2026, the party voted to begin negotiations to join the [[European Conservatives and Reformists Party]].<ref name=":7">{{cite web |title=Center Party urges fuel VAT cut, votes to join European Conservatives | url=https://news.err.ee/1609967648/center-party-urges-fuel-vat-cut-votes-to-join-european-conservatives |website=ERR News |access-date=14 March 2026}}</ref>

lead.Eurogroup:
{{Short description|Informal body of ministers of the eurozone member states}}
{{Use British English|date=March 2013}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=October 2020}}
{{Politics of the European Union}}

The '''Eurogroup''' is the recognised collective term for the informal meetings of the [[finance minister]]s of the [[eurozone]]—those [[member states of the European Union]] (EU) which have adopted the [[euro]] as their official [[currency]]. The group has 21 members. It exercises political control over the currency and related aspects of the [[Economic and Monetary Union of the European Union|EU's monetary union]] such as the [[Stability and Growth Pact]].

The ministers meet ''[[in camera]]'' a day before a meeting of the [[Economic and Financial Affairs Council]] (ECOFIN) of the [[Council of the European Union]]. They communicate their decisions via press and document releases.<ref>[http://www.eurozone.europa.eu/eurogroup Eurogroup] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141229234925/http://www.eurozone.europa.eu/eurogroup |date=29 December 2014 }} on eurozone.europa.eu.</ref> The group is related to the [[Council of the European Union]] (only Eurogroup member states vote on issues relating to the euro in the ECOFIN) and was formalised under the [[Treaty of Lisbon]].

