The '''Joint List''' ({{langx|ar|القائمة المشتركة|al-Qaʾimah al-Mushtarakah}}; {{langx|he|הָרְשִׁימָה הַמְּשֻׁתֶּפֶת|HaReshima HaMeshutefet}}) is a [[political alliance]] of [[Arab-Israeli|Arab]]-majority political parties in Israel. The current Joint List reestablished in 2026 by [[Hadash]], [[Balad (political party)|Balad]] and [[Ta'al]] adheres to a [[Left-wing politics|left-wing]], [[Progressivism|progressive]] and [[Anti-Zionism|Anti-Zionist]] platform.<ref name="ts"/> Originally it included the Islamist [[Ra'am]] party ahead of the [[2015 Israeli legislative election|2015 election]], but the original Joint List was dissolved in 2019 and 2021 due to the withdrawal of Ta'al and Ra'am, respectively.<ref name="ecfr.eu">https://ecfr.eu/special/mapping_palestinian_politics/joint_list/</ref>

The alliance was the third-largest faction in the [[List of members of the twentieth Knesset|Knesset]] after the [[2015 Israeli legislative election|2015 election]], estimated to have received 82% of the Arab vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.jpost.com/Israel-Elections/Arab-sector-turnout-for-recent-elections-reached-635-percent-polling-data-shows-394878|title=Arab sector turnout for recent elections reached 63.5%, polling data shows|work=[[The Jerusalem Post]]|first=Ariel |last=Ben Solomon|date=24 March 2015|access-date=2 April 2026}}</ref> In January 2019, [[Ta'al]] split from the alliance, and the remaining coalition was dissolved on 21 February 2019.<ref>{{cite news |title=Hadash and Ta'al Arab Parties join forces ahead of elections |url=https://www.ynetnews.com/articles/0,7340,L-5467724,00.html |work=[[Yedioth Ahronoth]] |date=21 February 2019|first=Hassan |last=Shaalan}}</ref> The Joint List was reestablished on 28 July for the [[September 2019 Israeli legislative election|September 2019 election]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/nationalist-balad-party-announces-it-will-run-on-joint-list-in-autumn-elections/|title=Nationalist Balad party announces it will run on Joint List in autumn elections|date=29 July 2019|work=[[The Times of Israel]]|first=Adam |last=Rasgon}}</ref> in which it won 13 seats and was again the third-largest faction.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/final-votes-being-tallied-with-likud-blue-and-white-still-neck-and-neck/|title=Final votes being tallied with Likud, Blue and White still neck-and-neck|author=Staff writer|website=[[The Times of Israel]]|date=19 September 2019}}</ref> In the [[2020 Israeli legislative election|2020 elections]], the Joint List increased its seats from 13 to 15, described by ''[[Haaretz]]'' as "an unprecedented showing."<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.haaretz.com/israel-news/elections/2020-03-06/ty-article/.premium/israel-election-2020-netanyahu-gantz/0000017f-e288-d38f-a57f-e6da17510000|title=Final Election Results: Netanyahu Bloc Short of Majority With 58 Seats|author=Staff writer|website=[[Haaretz]]|date=5 March 2020}}</ref> In the buildup to the [[2021 Israeli legislative election|2021 elections]], the Islamic conservative-leaning Ra'am left the Joint List due to ideological disagreements and ran on its own, gaining four seats, while the seats held by the Joint List fell to six. The alliance broke up prior to the [[2022 Israeli legislative election|2022 elections]], with Hadash and Ta'al running together and Balad running alone.

Hadash, Ta'al and Balad reached an agreement, following extensive negotiations, on 19 August 2026 to run together in the [[2026 Israeli legislative election]], while Ra'am chose to run separately. The slate was headed by Hadash chairman [[Yousef Jabareen]], followed by Ta'al chairman [[Ahmad Tibi]] and Balad chairman [[Sami Abu Shehadeh]]. After Shehadeh's withdrawal on 2 October, the third spot on the list was occupied by Hadash member [[Faten Ghattas]], while the highest placing Balad member was [[Bakhar Awawdeh]] at fourth. The alliance won 11 seats.

