The '''Kingdom of the Netherlands''',{{efn|{{langx|nl|Koninkrijk der Nederlanden}}, {{IPA|nl|ˈkoːnɪŋkrɛiɡ dɛr ˈneːdərlɑndə(n)|pron|Nl-Koninkrijk der Nederlanden2.ogg}};,{{efn|name=pronunciation|In isolation, {{lang|nl|Koninkrijk}} is pronounced {{IPA|nl|ˈkoːnɪŋkrɛik|}}.}} {{langx|fy|Keninkryk fan de Nederlannen}}, {{langx|pap|Reino Hulandes}}}} commonly known simply as the '''Netherlands''',{{efn|name=name|Not to be confused with the constituent country, the [[Netherlands]], which is only a part of the Kingdom. In some contexts "the Netherlands" refers to the constituent country, in more formal contexts it may refer to the Kingdom.}} is a [[sovereign state]] consisting of a collection of constituent territories united under the [[monarch of the Netherlands]], who functions as [[head of state]]. The realm is a [[unitary monarchy]] with its largest subdivision, the eponymous [[Netherlands]], predominantly located in [[Northwestern Europe]] and with three smaller island territories located in the [[Caribbean]].

The four subdivisions of the Kingdom—[[Aruba]], [[Curaçao]], the [[Netherlands]], and [[Sint Maarten]]—are [[Autonomous administrative division#Dutch constituent countries|constituent countries]] ({{lang|nl|landen}} in [[Dutch language|Dutch]]; {{singular}} {{lang|nl|land}}) and participate on a basis of equality as partners in the Kingdom.<ref>The Charter of the Kingdom was fully explained in an "Explanatory Memorandum to the Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands", transmitted to the [[United Nations Secretary-General]] in compliance with the wishes expressed in General Assembly resolutions 222 (III) and 747 (VIII) on 30 March 1955. ("Suriname en de Nederlandse Antillen in de Verenigde Naties", Publication No. 41 of the Netherlands Ministry of Foreign Affairs, ''[[Sdu (publishing company)|Staatsdrukkerij en uitgeversbedrijf]]'', 1956)</ref> All four are autonomous with their own [[parliament]]s, however, the Kingdom itself is administered in the constituent country of the Netherlands, which comprises roughly 98% of the Kingdom's land area and population, with matters like [[foreign policy]] and [[Military|defence]] done on behalf of the entire Kingdom.

The vast majority of land area of the constituent country of the Netherlands is in Europe, while its three [[Caribbean Netherlands|special municipalities]] ([[Bonaire]], [[Saba (island)|Saba]], and [[Sint Eustatius]]) are located in the Caribbean, as are the other three constituent countries.

