The '''Left Alliance'''{{efn|{{langx|fi|Vasemmistoliitto}} {{IPA|fi|ˈʋɑsemːistoˌliːtːo|}}, '''Vas'''; {{langx|sv|Vänsterförbundet}}, '''VF'''}} is a [[left-wing]]{{refn|<ref>{{cite web |title= Finland |url= https://europeelects.eu/finland/ |website= Europe Elects |access-date= 24 January 2025}}</ref><ref name= "Lloyds">{{cite web |title= Finland: Economic and Political Overview |url= https://www.lloydsbanktrade.com/en/market-potential/finland/political-context |website= Lloyds Bank |access-date= 24 January 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date= 14 June 2022 |title= Finland's Left Alliance ditches negative stance on Nato |url= https://www.helsinkitimes.fi/finland/finland-news/politics/21722-finland-s-left-alliance-ditches-negative-stance-on-nato.html |work= Helsinki Times |access-date= 24 January 2025}}</ref>}} [[list of political parties in Finland|political party in Finland]].<ref>{{bulleted list|{{cite book|author=Claire Annesley|title=Political and Economic Dictionary of Western Europe|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RefH7Ya5kU4C&pg=PT22|date=11 January 2013|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-135-35547-0|pages=22–}}|{{Cite journal|last=Raunio|first=Tapio|date=2010|title=The EU and the Welfare State are Compatible: Finnish Social Democrats and European Integration|url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0017257X00000099/type/journal_article|journal=Government and Opposition|language=en|volume=45|issue=2|pages=187–207|doi=10.1111/j.1477-7053.2009.01310.x|s2cid=154015109|issn=0017-257X|url-access=subscription}}|{{Cite book|last=Iso-Markku|first=Tuomas|date=May 2014|title=Linking domestic and European politics: Finnish MEPs and the votes that shaped the 7th European Parliament|url=https://www.fiia.fi/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/wp81.pdf|publisher=The Finnish Institute of International Affairs|isbn=978-951-769-414-8}}|{{Cite book|last=Raunio|first=Tapio|title=The Palgrave Handbook of National Parliaments and the European Union |date=January 2016|chapter=The Finnish Eduskunta and the European Union: The Strengths and Weaknesses of a Mandating System|pages=406–424 |chapter-url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/306217843|doi=10.1007/978-1-137-28913-1_20 |isbn=978-1-349-67095-6 }}|{{Cite web|date=2011-06-17|title=6-party government in Finland after lengthy talks|url=https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/sdut-6-party-government-in-finland-after-lengthy-talks-2011jun17-story.html|access-date=21 December 2021|website=San Diego Union-Tribune|language=en-US}}}}</ref>

The Left Alliance was founded in 1990 as the chief successor of the left-wing [[Finnish People's Democratic League]] (SKDL). Although not as electorally successful as the SKDL, it has achieved some success, typically receiving around eight to ten percent of the vote in parliamentary elections. It has participated in five cabinets, most recently in the [[Marin Cabinet]] from [[2019 Finnish parliamentary election|2019]] to [[2023 Finnish parliamentary election|2023]]. It is [[Socialism|socialist]],<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Matthews-Ferrero|first1=Daniel|last2=Päri|first2=Jasmin|last3=Steenland|first3=Robert|date=2019-05-10|title=EU country briefing: Finland|url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/eu-elections-2019/news/eu-country-briefing-finland/|access-date=2021-12-21|website=www.euractiv.com|language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Why Basic Income Failed in Finland |url=https://jacobin.com/2019/12/basic-income-finland-experiment-kela |access-date=2022-12-28 |website=jacobin.com |language=en-US}}</ref> specifically [[Democratic socialism|democratically socialist]],<ref>{{Cite web|date=September 2017|title=Populism Report|url=https://progressivepost.eu/wp-content/uploads/ps_q3-1.pdf|website=Foundation for European Progressive Studies}}</ref> and it supports the principles of [[eco-socialism]].<ref name="Nordsieck">{{cite web|last=Nordsieck|first=Wolfram|date=2019|title=Finland|url=http://parties-and-elections.eu/finland.html|access-date=8 May 2020|website=Parties and Elections in Europe}}</ref><ref name="Left Alliance Party Program 2007">{{cite web|date=2007|title=Left Alliance Party Program 2007|url=http://www.vasemmisto.fi/images/ohjelmat/Left_Alliance_Party_Program_2007.pdf|access-date=8 May 2020|website=Vasemmisto|archive-date=4 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200404182255/http://www.vasemmisto.fi/images/ohjelmat/Left_Alliance_Party_Program_2007.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The Left Alliance is a member of the [[Nordic Green Left Alliance]]. The party's newspaper is the monthly ''[[Kansan Uutiset]]''. It had two ministerial positions in the [[Marin cabinet]], [[Li Andersson]] as [[Ministry of Education and Culture (Finland)|Minister of Education]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Minister of Education |url=https://valtioneuvosto.fi/en/marin/minister-of-education |access-date=2022-06-14 |website=Valtioneuvosto |language=en-US |archive-date=2020-12-17 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217150127/https://valtioneuvosto.fi/en/marin/minister-of-education |url-status=dead }}</ref> and [[Hanna Sarkkinen]] as Minister of Social Affairs and Health.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Minister of Social Affairs and Health |url=https://valtioneuvosto.fi/en/marin/minister-of-social-affairs-and-health |access-date=14 June 2022 |website=Valtioneuvosto |language=en-US }}{{Dead link|date=August 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> It is also a part of the [[European Left Alliance for the People and the Planet]], a [[European political party]] that supports an alternative to capitalism.<ref>[https://leftalliance.eu/ WHO WE ARE]</ref>

