'''Renaud Lavillenie''' ({{IPA|fr|ʁə.no la.vi.lə.ni}} or {{IPA|fr|ʁə.no la.vil.ni|}}; born 18 September 1986) is a French [[pole vault]]er.
Lavillenie won the [[gold medal]] at the [[2012 Summer Olympics|2012 Olympics in London]] and the [[silver medal]] at the [[2016 Summer Olympics|2016 Olympics in Rio]]. In addition to his Olympic success, he has won three [[IAAF World Indoor Championships in Athletics|World Indoor Championships]] gold medals, three [[European Athletics Championships|European Championships]] gold medals and four [[European Athletics Indoor Championships|European Indoor Championships]] gold medals. He has also won one silver medal and four [[bronze medal]]s at the [[IAAF World Championships in Athletics|World Championships]].<ref name=bio>{{World Athletics}}</ref><ref name="francerecord">{{cite web |url=http://www.iaaf.org/news/kind=100/newsid=59467.html |title=Lavillenie's 6.03m clearance dazzles Paris |publisher=[[International Association of Athletics Federations]] |date=5 March 2011 |access-date=6 March 2011 |archive-date=7 March 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110307022210/http://www.iaaf.org/news/kind=100/newsid=59467.html |url-status=live}}</ref> As of 25 August 2016, he holds the French [[French records in athletics|national records]] for the highest pole vault clearance both outdoors (6.05 m) and indoors (6.16 m). The 6.16 was the absolute world record for the pole vault for over six years, 2014–2020.  He was the pole vault overall winner of the [[IAAF Diamond League]] in seven consecutive years, from 2010 to 2016.

Outside pole vaulting, Lavillenie is a keen motorcyclist, and raced in the 2013 [[24 Hours of Le Mans (motorcycle race)|Le Mans 24 Hours for motorcycles]], finishing 25th. Lavillenie subsequently entered the 2014 race, aiming for a top 20 finish.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.la-croix.com/Actualite/Sport/Lavillenie-aux-24-heures-du-Mans-Pourquoi-pas-un-Top-20-2014-08-27-1197682 |title=Lavillenie aux 24 heures du Mans: "Pourquoi pas un Top 20" |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=27 August 2014 |website=[[La Croix (newspaper)|La Croix]] |language=fr |trans-title=Lavillenie at the 24 Hours of Le Mans: "Why not a Top 20" |access-date=5 September 2014 |archive-date=6 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140906042152/http://www.la-croix.com/Actualite/Sport/Lavillenie-aux-24-heures-du-Mans-Pourquoi-pas-un-Top-20-2014-08-27-1197682 |url-status=live}}</ref> Lavillenie's younger brother [[Valentin Lavillenie]] is also a pole vaulter.

