The '''Republican Party''', also known as the '''Grand Old Party''' ('''GOP'''), is a political party in the United States, sitting on the [[Right-wing politics|right-wing]] to [[Far-right politics|far-right]] of the [[political spectrum]].<!--Do not alter "right-wing to far-right" designation without RFC.--> Founded in 1854, it emerged as the main rival of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], and the two parties have dominated [[American politics]] since then.

The Republican Party was founded by [[anti-slavery]] activists who opposed the [[Kansas–Nebraska Act]] and the expansion of [[slavery in the United States|slavery into American territories]]. It rapidly gained support in the [[Northern United States|North]], drawing in former [[Whig Party (United States)|Whigs]], [[Free Soil Party|Free Soilers]], and former [[Know Nothing]]s. [[Abraham Lincoln]]'s [[1860 United States presidential election|election in 1860]] as president led to the secession of Southern states and the outbreak of the [[American Civil War]]. Under Lincoln and a Republican-controlled Congress, the party led efforts to preserve the [[Union (American Civil War)|Union]], defeat the [[Confederate States of America|Confederacy]], and [[End of slavery in the United States|abolish slavery]]. During the [[Reconstruction era]], Republicans sought to extend [[civil rights]] protections to [[freedmen]], and by the late 1870s, the party shifted its focus toward business interests and [[industrialization in the United States|industrial expansion]]. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, it dominated national politics, promoting [[protective tariff]]s, infrastructure development, and ''[[laissez-faire]]'' economic policies, while navigating internal divisions between [[Progressive Era|progressive]] and conservative [[Factions in the Republican Party (United States)|factions]]. The party's support declined during the [[Great Depression]], as the [[New Deal coalition]] reshaped American politics. Republicans returned to national power with the [[1952 United States presidential election|1952 election]] of [[Dwight D. Eisenhower]], whose [[Liberal conservatism|moderate conservatism]] reflected a pragmatic acceptance of many [[New Deal]]-era programs.

Following the [[civil rights era]], the Republican Party's use of the [[Southern strategy]] appealed to many white voters disaffected by Democratic support for civil rights, and ''[[de facto]]'' flipping two of the parties' [[Voting bloc|voting blocs]] ([[African Americans]] and [[White Southerners]]). The [[1980 United States presidential election|1980 election]] of [[Ronald Reagan]] as president [[Political realignment|realigned]] national politics, consolidating [[Reagan coalition|a coalition]] of [[free market]] advocates, [[Social conservatism in the United States|social conservatives]] and foreign policy [[war hawk|hawks]] under the Republican banner. Since 2009, a shift toward [[right-wing populism]] culminated in the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 election]] of [[Donald Trump]] as president, whose leadership style and political agenda—often referred to as [[Trumpism]]—reshaped the party's identity and [[Political positions of the Republican Party (United States)|political platform]].

The present-day Republican Party is primarily [[Right-wing populism|right-wing populist]] and [[Neo-nationalism|neo-nationalist]], with smaller [[Conservatism in the United States|conservative]], [[Christian right]], and [[Libertarianism in the United States|libertarian]] factions, all of which have greatly diminished in influence since 2016. Its ideology has largely shifted towards [[Illiberal democracy|illiberalism]], favoring strong [[Presidential system|presidential]] power<ref name="Lührmann-2020" /><ref name="Roads to Congress 2024"/><ref name="Illib2">{{Cite news |date=January 23, 2026 |title=How President Trump has challenged a constitutional foundation |url=https://www.npr.org/2026/01/23/g-s1-106562/trump-democracy-constitution-executive-power |access-date=May 9, 2026 |work=NPR |language=en |quote=In his first year back in the White House, President Trump has presided over a sweeping expansion of executive power while eroding democratic norms. Many scholars of democracy say that these moves are unprecedented in U.S. history and that Trump has pushed the United States toward authoritarianism.}}</ref> and a [[Statism|statist]] government to enforce [[Social conservatism in the United States|conservative cultural values]].<ref name="Roads to Congress 2024">{{Cite book |last1=Tyler Godines Camarillo |first1=C. |title=The Roads to Congress 2024: Navigating Elections in an Era of Upheaval |last2=Clark Wilson |first2=Walter |last3=Perroud |first3=Jace |last4=VanGoethem |first4=Margaret |date=January 3, 2026 |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan Cham |isbn=978-3-032-07119-4 |pages=127–151 |chapter=The New Republican Revolution: The Implications of Illiberalism and Populism for GOP Politics and Policy}}</ref> This includes [[United States anti-abortion movement|broad opposition to abortion]], <!-- Do not misrepresent Heather Wyatt-Nichol's "Political Attacks on the Lgbtqia+ Community" by putting "some" in the sentence or citation-->[[LGBTQ rights in the United States|LGBT rights]], [[Immigration to the United States|immigration]], and [[Gun control in the United States|gun control]]. On economic policy, it supports [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|tariffs]] while opposing [[Free trade|free trade]] and [[Labor unions in the United States|labor unions]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/22/world/canada/carney-tariffs-trade-trump.html|title='Attacked' by Trump on Trade, Canada Is 'at War' With U.S., Carney Says|first=Ian|last=Austen|date=22 August 2026|access-date=22 August 2026|website=The New York Times}}</ref>

During the 20th and early 21st centuries, the Republican Party has cooperated internationally with [[Conservatism|conservative]] parties. Since the 2020s it has aligned with global far-right parties,<ref name="Colom-2024" /><ref name="Tharoor-2021" /><ref name="Sunshine-2026" /> while [[White nationalism|white nationalist]] beliefs have become influential within the party,{{refn|name=WhiteNat}} including support for [[remigration]].<ref name="Kneeland-2016" /><ref name="Shamim 20260506"/><ref name="Yousef-2025" /><ref name="Tharoor 20251130"/>

