{{Western Sahara conflict}}
The '''Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic''' ('''SADR'''),{{Efn|{{IPAc-en|s|ə|ˈ|r|ɑː|w|i}} {{respell|sə|RAH|wee}}; SADR; also known as ''Saharawi Arab Democratic Republic''; {{langx|ar|الجمهورية العربية الصحراوية الديمقراطية|al-Jumhūriyyah al-ʿArabiyyah aṣ-Ṣaḥrāwiyyah ad-Dīmuqrāṭiyyah|engvar=gb}}; {{langx|es|República Árabe Saharaui Democrática}}, {{IPA|es|reˈpuβlik(a) ˈaɾaβe sa(χa)ˈɾawi ðemoˈkɾatika||Pronunciation_of_República_Árabe_Saharaui_Democrática_in_Spanish.ogg}}}} also known as the '''Sahrawi Republic''', is a [[International recognition of the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic|partially recognised state]] in the western [[Maghreb]] that claims sovereignty over the entire [[United Nations list of non-self-governing territories|non-self-governing territory]] of [[Western Sahara]], but controls only [[Free Zone (region)|the easternmost portion of that territory]].

Its claimed capital city is [[Laayoune]], which is also the largest city in Western Sahara. Since the SADR does not control Laayoune, it has established a [[temporary capital]] in [[Tifariti]], although most of the day-to-day administration happens in [[Rabouni refugee camp|Rabuni]], one of the [[Sahrawi refugee camps]] located in [[Tindouf]], Algeria.

The SADR was proclaimed by the [[Polisario Front]] on 27 February 1976, in [[Bir Lehlou]], Western Sahara. The SADR government calls the territories under its control the ''Liberated Territories'' or the ''[[Free Zone (region)|Free Zone]]''. [[Morocco]] occupies the rest of the disputed territory, and calls these lands its [[Moroccan-occupied Western Sahara|Southern Provinces]]. The SADR is a full member of the [[African Union]], but is not a member of the [[Arab League]], nor of the [[Arab Maghreb Union]]. It is one of only two African states in which the [[Spanish language]] is a significant language, the other being [[Equatorial Guinea]].

