'''Siemens AG''' ({{IPA|de|ˈziːməns|-|De-Siemens.ogg}}<ref>{{cite book |author1=Dudenredaktion |last2=Kleiner |first2=Stefan |last3=Knöbl |first3=Ralf |year=2015|orig-year=First published 1962 |title=Das Aussprachewörterbuch|trans-title=The Pronunciation Dictionary |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=T6vWCgAAQBAJ |language=de |edition=7th |location=Berlin |publisher=Dudenverlag |isbn=978-3-411-04067-4}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Krech |first1=Eva-Maria |last2=Stock |first2=Eberhard |last3=Hirschfeld |first3=Ursula |last4=Anders |first4=Lutz Christian |title=Deutsches Aussprachewörterbuch|trans-title=German Pronunciation Dictionary |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=E-1tr_oVkW4C&q=deutsches+ausspracheworterbuch |language=de |year=2009 |publisher=Walter de Gruyter |location=Berlin |isbn=978-3-11-018202-6}}</ref><ref name="LPD">{{cite book |last=Wells |first=John C. |year=2008 |title=Longman Pronunciation Dictionary |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gXFAngEACAAJ&q=longman+pronunciation+dictionary |edition=3rd |publisher=Longman |isbn=9781405881180|access-date=2026-09-04}}</ref> {{small|or}} {{IPA|de|-mɛns|}}<ref name="LPD"/>) is a German [[Multinational corporation|multinational]] company. It is focused on [[Industrial control system|industrial automation]], [[building automation]], [[rail transport]] and [[health technology]].<ref name="FY2022">{{cite web |title=Siemens Report for Fiscal 2022 |url=https://www.siemens.com/applications/b09c49eb-3a14-73b3-9f71-e30e3c2dfdbd/s3_assets/pdfs/en/Siemens_Report_FY2022.pdf?ste_sid=172f405796c38fe14a51d77f45492b39 |access-date=30 January 2022 |publisher=Siemens}}</ref> Siemens is the largest engineering company in Europe,<ref name=":0">{{cite news |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-11-07/siemens-expects-2020-decline-in-some-businesses-amid-car-slump |title=Siemens Quarterly Profit Surge Comes With Cautious Outlook |last=Sachgau |first=Oliver |newspaper=Bloomberg.com|date=7 November 2019 |access-date=7 November 2019}}</ref> and holds the position of global market leader in industrial [[Artificial intelligence|AI]], automation, and industrial [[software]].<ref name="Handelsblatt">{{Cite web |first=Axel |last=Höpner| title=Siemens: Chef Roland Busch sieht enormes Potenzial in den USA |url=https://www.handelsblatt.com/unternehmen/industrie/siemens-chef-roland-busch-sieht-enormes-potenzial-in-den-usa/28977580.html |access-date=2023-07-07 |website=[[Handelsblatt]] |language=de}}</ref>

The origins of the conglomerate can be traced back to 1847 to the ''Telegraphen Bau-Anstalt von Siemens & Halske'' established in [[Berlin]] by [[Werner von Siemens]] and [[Johann Georg Halske]]. In 1966, the present-day corporation emerged from the merger of three companies: [[Siemens & Halske]], [[Siemens-Schuckert]], and [[Siemens-Reiniger-Werke]]. Today headquartered in [[Munich]] and Berlin, Siemens and its subsidiaries employ approximately 320,000 people worldwide and reported a global revenue of around €78 billion in 2023.<ref name="FY2022" /> The company is a component of the [[DAX]] and [[Euro Stoxx 50]] stock market indices.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Frankfurt Stock Exchange |url=http://www.boerse-frankfurt.de/en/equities/indices/euro%2Bstoxx%2B50%2BEU0009658145/constituents |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151119171222/http://www.boerse-frankfurt.de/en/equities/indices/euro%2Bstoxx%2B50%2BEU0009658145/constituents |archive-date=2015-11-19 |access-date=2026-09-03 |website=Börse Frankfurt |language=en-EN}}</ref> As of December 2023, Siemens is the second largest German company by [[market capitalization]].<ref name="market cap">{{Cite news |date=2023-12-29 |title=Apple bleibt wertvollstes Unternehmen: SAP und Siemens in den Top 100 |url=https://www.faz.net/aktuell/wirtschaft/unternehmen/apple-bleibt-wertvollstes-unternehmen-sap-und-siemens-in-den-top-100-19413847.html |access-date=2023-12-29 |work=FAZ.NET |language=de |issn=0174-4909}}</ref>

As of 2023, the principal divisions of Siemens are Digital Industries, Smart Infrastructure, [[Siemens Mobility|Mobility]], and [[Siemens Financial Services|Financial Services]], with Siemens Mobility operating as an independent entity. Major business divisions that were once part of Siemens before being [[Corporate spin-off|spun off]] include [[semiconductor]] manufacturer [[Infineon Technologies]] (1999), [[Siemens Mobile]] (2005), [[Gigaset Communications]] (2008), the photonics business [[Osram]] (2013), [[Siemens Healthineers]] (2017), and [[Siemens Energy]] (2020).

