The '''Social Democratic Party of Finland''' ({{langx|fi|Suomen sosialidemokraattinen puolue}} {{IPA|fi|ˈsuo̯men ˈsosiɑ(ː)liˌdemokrɑːtːinen ˈpuo̯lue|}}, '''SDP'''; {{langx|sv|Finlands socialdemokratiska parti}}, '''SD'''), nicknamed '''''demarit''''' in Finnish, is a [[Social democracy|social democratic]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Information and Result Service |url=https://tulospalvelu.vaalit.fi/indexe.html |website=Vaalit: Information and Result Service}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Bale |first=Tim |title=Riding the populist wave: Europe's mainstream right in crisis |date=2021 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |others=Cristóbal Rovira Kaltwasser |isbn=978-1-009-00686-6 |location=Cambridge, United Kingdom |pages=35 |oclc=1256593260}}</ref> [[List of political parties in Finland|political party in Finland]]. It is the third-largest party in the [[Parliament of Finland]] with a total of 42 seats.

Founded in 1899 as the '''Workers' Party of Finland''' ({{langx|fi|link=no|Suomen työväenpuolue}}; {{langx|sv|link=no|Finlands arbetarparti}}) the SDP is Finland's oldest active political party and has a close relationship with the [[Central Organisation of Finnish Trade Unions]]. It is also a member of the [[Party of European Socialists]], [[Progressive Alliance]] and [[Socialist International]].

Following the resignation of [[Antti Rinne]] in December 2019, [[Sanna Marin]] became the country's 46th [[Prime Minister of Finland|prime minister]]. The SDP formed a new [[Marin Cabinet|coalition government]] on the basis of its predecessor, the [[Rinne Cabinet]], in effect continuing its cooperation with the [[Centre Party (Finland)|Centre Party]], [[Green League]], [[Left Alliance (Finland)|Left Alliance]] and [[Swedish People's Party of Finland|Swedish People's Party]]. Of the nineteen ministerial spots that were decided upon in conjunction, seven of them were designated to the SDP in the [[Marin Cabinet]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://valtioneuvosto.fi/en/rinne/ministers|title=Ministers|website=Valtioneuvosto|access-date=20 June 2019}}</ref> In September 2023, [[Antti Lindtman]] was elected as leader of the party following Marin's resignation after the [[2023 Finnish parliamentary election]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 September 2023|title=SDP elects Antti Lindtman as Sanna Marin's successor |url=https://yle.fi/a/74-20048213|access-date=18 February 2024|website=News|language=en}}</ref>

