The '''Socialist Party''' ({{langx|fr|Parti socialiste}} {{IPA|fr|paʁti sɔsjalist|}}, '''PS'''{{efn|{{IPA|fr|pe ɛs}}}}) is a [[Centre-left politics|centre-left]]<ref name="Statham2007">{{cite book|author=Paul Statham|chapter=Political communication and European integration and the transformation of national public spheres: a comparison of Britain and France|editor1=John Erik Fossum|editor2=Philip R. Schlesinger|title=The European Union and the Public Sphere: A Communicative Space in the Making?|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=s4K4BEeaJbQC&pg=PA127|year=2007|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-134-17462-1|page=127}}</ref><ref name="Topaloff2012">{{cite book|author=Liubomir K. Topaloff|title=Political Parties and Euroscepticism|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=69QtbwLss0oC&pg=PA171|year=2012|publisher=Palgrave Macmillan|isbn=978-0-230-36176-8|page=171}}</ref><ref name=Guardian>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2017/jan/29/french-socialists-leftwing-rebel-benoit-hamon-elysee-manuel-valls-francois-hollande-presidency|work=[[The Guardian]]|author=Chrisafis, Angelique|location=[[Paris]]|date=29 January 2017|title=French Socialists choose leftwing rebel Benoît Hamon for Élysée fight|archive-date=30 January 2017|access-date=28 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170130051343/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2017/jan/29/french-socialists-leftwing-rebel-benoit-hamon-elysee-manuel-valls-francois-hollande-presidency|url-status=live}}</ref> to [[Left-wing politics|left-wing]]<ref name="De La Cerda 2023"/><ref name="Public Senat 2022"/><ref name="France Info 2018"/><ref name="Jean Jaures 2023"/> [[List of political parties in France|political party]] in France. It holds [[Social democracy|social democratic]]<ref>The Parti Socialiste is widely described as social-democratic:
*{{cite book|editor1=Ari-Veikko Anttiroiko|editor2=Matti Mälkiä|title=Encyclopedia of Digital Government|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iDrTMazYhdkC&pg=PA397|year=2007|publisher=Idea Group Inc (IGI)|isbn=978-1-59140-790-4|page=397}}
*{{cite book|author=Dimitri Almeida|title=The Impact of European Integration on Political Parties: Beyond the Permissive Consensus|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oD7bKbo0FYEC&pg=PT71|year=2012|publisher=CRC Press|isbn=978-1-136-34039-0|page=71}}
*{{cite book|author1=Richard Collin|author2=Pamela L. Martin|title=An Introduction to World Politics: Conflict and Consensus on a Small Planet|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-vSlx-_Z408C&pg=PA218|year=2012|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=978-1-4422-1803-1|page=218}}
*{{cite book|author1=Hans-Peter Kriesi|author2=Edgar Grande|author3=Martin Dolezal|author4=Marc Helbling|author5=Dominic Höglinger|author6=Swen Hutter|author7=Bruno Wüest|title=Political Conflict in Western Europe|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=6_zivrfDd3AC&pg=PA52|year=2012|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=978-1-107-02438-0|page=52}}
*{{cite book|author1=Wolfgang Merkel|author2=Alexander Petring|author3=Christian Henkes|author4=Christoph Egle|title=Social Democracy in Power: The Capacity to Reform|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ek99AgAAQBAJ|year=2008|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-134-07178-4}}</ref> and [[Pro-Europeanism|pro-European]] views.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://europeelects.eu/france/|title=France|access-date=29 December 2021|archive-date=20 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200820011956/https://europeelects.eu/european-union/france/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref> {{Cite web |title=Our national parties {{!}} Socialists & Democrats |url=https://www.socialistsanddemocrats.eu/who-we-are/our-political-family/our-national-parties |access-date=2024-06-15 |website=www.socialistsanddemocrats.eu |language=en}}</ref> The PS was for decades the largest party of the "[[French Left]]" and used to be one of the two major political parties under the [[French Fifth Republic|Fifth Republic]], along with the [[Rally for the Republic]] in the late 20th century, and with the [[Union for a Popular Movement]] in the early 2000s. It is currently led by First Secretary [[Olivier Faure]]. The PS is a member of the [[Party of European Socialists]], [[Progressive Alliance]] and [[Socialist International]].

The PS was founded in 1969 from a merger of the [[French Section of the Workers' International]] (SFIO), the [[Convention of Republican Institutions]] led by [[François Mitterrand]], and other groups. In the 1970s, the PS surpassed the [[French Communist Party|Communist Party]]'s share of the left-wing vote. It first won power in 1981, when Mitterrand was elected [[president of France|president]]. The PS achieved a governing majority in the [[French National Assembly|National Assembly]] from 1981 to 1986, and again from 1988 to 1993. PS leader [[Lionel Jospin]] lost his bid to succeed Mitterrand as president in 1995 to conservative [[Jacques Chirac]], but he served as [[Prime Minister of France|prime minister]] in a [[Cohabitation (government)|cohabitation government]] from 1997 to 2002, when he was again defeated by Chirac in the presidential election.

In the [[2007 French presidential election|2007 presidential election]], the PS's candidate, [[Ségolène Royal]], was defeated by conservative [[Nicolas Sarkozy]]. In 2012, [[François Hollande]], the leader of the party from 1997 to 2008, was elected president, and the party also won a governing majority. During his term, Hollande battled with high unemployment, [[List of terrorist incidents in France|multiple Jihadi terrorist attacks]], poor opinion ratings and a splinter group of Socialist MPs known as ''frondeurs'' (rebels). Facing the emergence of centrist [[Emmanuel Macron]] and left-winger [[Jean-Luc Mélenchon]], PS candidate [[Benoît Hamon]] finished 5th in the [[2017 French presidential election|2017 presidential election]]. The PS also declined to the 4th largest party in the [[2017 French legislative election|2017 legislative election]], and to the 6th largest in [[2022 French legislative election|2022]].

Several figures who acted at the international level have also been members of the PS, including [[Jacques Delors]], who was the [[president of the European Commission]] from 1985 to 1994 and the first person to serve three terms in that office;<ref name="europa1">{{citation | title = European Commission – Discover the former Presidents | url = http://ec.europa.eu/commission_barroso/president/history/delors/index_en.htm | access-date = 21 September 2009 | archive-date = 23 October 2007 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20071023032747/http://ec.europa.eu/commission_barroso/president/history/delors/index_en.htm | url-status = live }}</ref> [[Dominique Strauss-Kahn]], who was the [[managing director]] of the [[International Monetary Fund]] from 2007 to 2011;<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.imf.org/external/np/exr/chron/mds.asp|title=IMF Managing Directors|access-date=20 June 2012|archive-date=8 September 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180908225847/https://www.imf.org/external/np/exr/chron/mds.asp|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Pascal Lamy]], who was [[Director-General of the World Trade Organization|Director-General]] of the [[World Trade Organization]] from 2005 to 2013.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/dg_e/exdgs_e.htm |title=Previous GATT and WTO Directors-General |publisher=WTO |access-date=20 June 2012 |archive-date=31 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200531140433/https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/dg_e/exdgs_e.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Party membership has declined precipitously, to a low of 22,000 members in 2021,<ref name="membership">{{Cite web|url = https://www.francetvinfo.fr/politique/ps/olivier-faure-reelu-a-la-tete-du-parti-socialiste_4774323.html|title = Olivier Faure réélu à la tête du Parti socialiste|date = 17 September 2021|access-date = 10 April 2022|archive-date = 17 September 2021|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210917050848/https://www.francetvinfo.fr/politique/ps/olivier-faure-reelu-a-la-tete-du-parti-socialiste_4774323.html|url-status = live}}</ref> down from 173,486 members in 2012,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.parti-socialiste.fr/static/8455/documents/harlem-desir-et-emmanuel-maurel-candidats-au-poste-de-premier-secretaire.pdf|title=PS: Résultats officiels validés|publisher=Socialist Party|date=12 October 2012|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131101034540/http://www.parti-socialiste.fr/static/8455/documents/harlem-desir-et-emmanuel-maurel-candidats-au-poste-de-premier-secretaire.pdf|archive-date=1 November 2013}}</ref> although it claimed 47,000 members in 2024,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.sudouest.fr/france/le-parti-socialiste-lance-une-campagne-d-adhesion-et-revendique-47-000-membres-22196637.php |title=Le Parti socialiste lance une campagne d'adhésion et revendique 47&nbsp;000 membres |access-date=21 November 2024 |archive-date=18 November 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241118221120/https://www.sudouest.fr/france/le-parti-socialiste-lance-une-campagne-d-adhesion-et-revendique-47-000-membres-22196637.php |url-status=live }}</ref> recovering to its standing around 2016, when it had 42,300 members.<ref name="2017-members">{{cite news|author=Ludovic Galtier|title=Parti socialiste : 42.300 adhérents seraient à jour de cotisation|url=http://www.rtl.fr/actu/politique/parti-socialiste-42-300-adherents-seraient-a-jour-de-cotisation-7786053599|publisher=RTL|date=30 November 2016|archive-date=7 February 2017|access-date=6 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170207191320/http://www.rtl.fr/actu/politique/parti-socialiste-42-300-adherents-seraient-a-jour-de-cotisation-7786053599|url-status=live}}</ref>

