'''South Ossetia''',{{efn|Pronounced {{IPAc-en|ɒ|ˈ|s|ɛ|t|i|ə|audio=Ossetia pronunciation.mp3}} {{respell|o|SET|ee|ə}}, {{small|less commonly}} {{IPAc-en|ɒ|ˈ|s|iː|ʃ|ə|audio=Ossetia alternate pronunciation.mp3}} {{respell|o|SEE|shə}};<ref>[https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/ossetia "Ossetia"] {{Webarchive |url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181023120304/https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/ossetia |date=23 October 2018 }}. ''[[Collins English Dictionary]]''.</ref> {{langx|os|Хуссар Ирыстон|Xussar Iryston|engvar=gb}}; {{langx|ru|Южная Осетия|Yuzhnaya Osetiya|engvar=gb}}; {{langx|ka|სამხრეთ ოსეთი|Samkhret Oseti}}.}} formally the '''Republic of South Ossetia—the State of Alania'''<ref>
* {{Cite news |last1=Fuller |first1=Liz |date=9 February 2017 |title=South Ossetia Referendum on Name Change Steers Clear of Thornier Unification Issue |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/caucasus-report-south-ossetia-referendum-name-change/28298590.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170914173029/https://www.rferl.org/a/caucasus-report-south-ossetia-referendum-name-change/28298590.html |archive-date=14 September 2017 |access-date=23 July 2018 |newspaper=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty |language=en}}
* {{Cite web |date=7 February 2017 |title=Referendum to rename South Ossetia 'Alania' set for April |url=http://oc-media.org/referendum-to-rename-south-ossetia-alania-set-for-april/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180724032235/http://oc-media.org/referendum-to-rename-south-ossetia-alania-set-for-april/ |archive-date=24 July 2018 |access-date=23 July 2018 |website=[[OC Media]] |language=en-US}}</ref> since 2017,<ref name=":1" /> and originally the '''Republic of South Ossetia''', is a [[List of states with limited recognition|partially recognised state]] in the [[South Caucasus]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Foltz |first=Richard |title=The Ossetes: Modern-Day Scythians of the Caucasus. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YZswEAAAQBAJ |year=2022 |location=London |publisher=Bloomsbury |isbn=9780755618453 |author-link=Richard Foltz |pages=123–144 |access-date=3 January 2022 |archive-date=18 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230218082625/https://books.google.com/books?id=YZswEAAAQBAJ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 March 2023 |title=South Ossetia profile |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-18269210 |access-date=31 May 2023 |website=BBC |archive-date=29 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180729082549/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-18269210 |url-status=live }}</ref> It has an officially stated population of just over 56,500 people (2022), who live in an area of {{convert|3,900|sqkm}}, with 33,000 living in the capital city, [[Tskhinvali]]. It ''de facto'' borders only Russia and Georgia.

As of 2026, four members of the [[United Nations]] (UN) recognise South Ossetia as a sovereign state{{snd}}[[Russia]], [[Venezuela]], [[Nicaragua]], and [[Syria]].<ref name="recognitions">{{cite web |date=2018-05-29 |title=Syria Recognizes Abkhazia, South Ossetia |url=https://civil.ge/archives/242795 |accessdate=2022-03-26 |work=Civil Georgia |archive-date=25 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220525185253/https://civil.ge/archives/242795 |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Government of Georgia (country)|Georgian government]] and all other UN member states regard South Ossetia as [[Westphalian system|sovereign territory]] of [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Robinson |first1=Matt |last2=Mchedlishvili |first2=Niko |date=24 October 2008 |title=Georgia seeks to isolate Russian-backed regions |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSLO408907 |access-date=31 May 2023 |archive-date=31 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531225544/https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSLO408907 |url-status=live }}</ref> Georgia<ref>{{cite news|date=31 October 2008|title=Bill on Occupied Territories Signed into Law|language=en |work=Civil Georgia|url=https://civil.ge/archives/117815|access-date=31 May 2023}}</ref> and the [[European Court of Human Rights]]{{efn|See [[Georgia v. Russia (II)]] and [[Malachini and Others v. Russia]]}}<ref>{{cite news |last=Seskuria |first=Natia|date=11 February 2021 |title=Georgia's historic victory: Implications of the ECHR ruling|language=en |work=Middle East Institute|url=https://mei.edu/publication/georgias-historic-victory-implications-echr-ruling/|access-date=31 May 2023|quote=Perhaps most importantly, the court established that Russia has been exercising “effective control” over the Tskhinvali region and Abkhazia since 12 Aug., 2008. This means the ECHR has legally demonstrated that Georgian territories are under Moscow’s ongoing occupation, and any further attempts of aggression will be linked directly to the Kremlin. Russia has also been held responsible for procedural violations regarding its failure to undergo an investigation into hostilities and human rights violations.}}</ref> have ruled the region to be under the [[Russian-occupied territories in Georgia|Russian occupation]] because of Russia's military, political and economic presence in South Ossetia.<ref>{{cite journal|date=2023|last=Cain|first=Edward|title=European Court of Human Rights' Ruling in Georgia v. Russia (II) and Its Application to the Current Crisis in Ukraine|language=en |journal=Loyola University Chicago International Law Review|url=https://lawecommons.luc.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1255&context=lucilr|volume=19|issue=2|p=216}}</ref>

The political status of South Ossetia is a central issue of the [[Georgian–Ossetian conflict]] and [[Georgia–Russia relations]]. The [[Government of Georgia (country)|Georgian government]] informally refers to the area as the '''Tskhinvali region'''{{efn|{{langx|ka|ცხინვალის რეგიონი|tskhinvalis regioni}}; {{langx|ru|Цхинвальский регион|Tskhinvalskiy region|engvar=gb}}}} and considers it a part of Georgia's [[Imereti]], [[Mtskheta-Mtianeti]], [[Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti]] and [[Shida Kartli]] regions. Georgia maintained local councils [[2006 Georgian local elections|elected in 2006]] in [[Akhalgori Municipality|Akhalgori]], [[Kurta Municipality|Kurta]], [[Tighva municipality|Tighva]] and [[Eredvi Municipality|Eredvi municipalities]] before they were captured by the separatists and Russia in 2008.<ref>{{cite news|date=17 November 2025 |title=Gov't moves to abolish administration created on territory of former South Ossetian Autonomous District, says Georgian Parliament Speaker |work=Imedi |url=https://info.imedi.ge/en/politics/7647/govt-moves-to-abolish-administration-created-on-territory-of-former-south-ossetian-autonomous-district-says-georgian-parliament-speaker|access-date=20 November 2025 }}</ref>

The South Ossetian Autonomous Oblast, established by Soviet authorities in Moscow in 1922, [[declared independence]] from the [[Georgian Soviet Socialist Republic]] in September 1990. Towards the end of 1990, the situation for ethnic Georgians in the region worsened sharply. There were multiple lootings and beatings committed both by Georgian and Ossetian armed forces and paramilitary units.<ref name="bloodshed">{{cite web |last=Human Rights Watch |title=Bloodshed in the Caucasus: Violations of humanitarian law in the Georgian-Ossetian Conflict |work=[[Human Rights Watch]] |url=https://www.hrw.org/reports/pdfs/g/georgia/georgia.923/georgia923full.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305212828/https://www.hrw.org/reports/pdfs/g/georgia/georgia.923/georgia923full.pdf |archive-date=5 March 2016 }}</ref> The Georgian government responded by abolishing South Ossetia's autonomy and dispatching its troops to the region.<ref name="icg-avoidwar" /> The escalating crisis led to the [[South Ossetia war (1991–1992)|1991–1992 South Ossetia War]] with Russian involvement on the Ossetian side.<ref name="CS">{{Cite web|url=https://www.csmonitor.com/1992/0622/22022.html|title=Russians Cope With Arc of Crises|website=[[The Christian Science Monitor]]|date=22 June 1992|access-date=23 November 2023|archive-date=18 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231118190027/https://www.csmonitor.com/1992/0622/22022.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Wash">{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1992/06/16/russian-threatens-georgia/74219b2f-cf48-4bfc-92f6-64364aa61f63/|title=Russian threatens Georgia|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=16 June 1992}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |author= Robert H. Donaldson |author2= Joseph L. Nogee |title= The Foreign Policy of Russia: Changing Systems, Enduring Interests |year=2005 |publisher=M.E. Sharpe |isbn= 9780765615688 |page=199}}</ref> After the war, the conflict remained [[Frozen conflict|frozen]] throughout the 1990s and saw two major escalations in the 2000s: in [[Georgian–Ossetian conflict#The 2004 flare-up|2004]] and in [[Georgian–Ossetian conflict#Events in 2008|2008]].<ref name="parl">{{cite web |url=http://www.parl.gc.ca/Content/LOP/ResearchPublications/prb0836-e.htm |title=The Conflict Between Russia and Georgia |author=Jean-Rodrigue Paré |publisher=Parliament of Canada |date=13 February 2009 |access-date=19 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160102030749/http://www.parl.gc.ca/Content/LOP/ResearchPublications/prb0836-e.htm |archive-date=2 January 2016 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name="king_fivedaywar">{{Cite journal |author= Charles King |url=http://faculty.georgetown.edu/kingch/King_Five_Day_War.pdf |title= The Five-Day War: Managing Moscow After the Georgia Crisis |publisher=Georgetown University |journal=Foreign Affairs |issue=November/December |date=2008 |access-date=22 June 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140923005750/http://faculty.georgetown.edu/kingch/King_Five_Day_War.pdf |archive-date=23 September 2014 |url-status=live}}</ref> The latter conflict led to the full-scale [[Russo-Georgian War]] of August 2008, during which Ossetian and Russian forces gained full ''de facto'' control of the territory of the former South Ossetian Autonomous Oblast. Georgia and most other states consider South Ossetia to be [[Russian-occupied territories in Georgia|under Russian military occupation]].

South Ossetia relies heavily on military, political, and financial aid from Russia.<ref name="Economy_Army">{{cite news |url= http://www.themoscowtimes.com/opinion/article/south-ossetia-looking-much-like-a-failed-state/408685.html |title=South Ossetia Looking Much Like a Failed State |agency=Associated Press |access-date=12 July 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100708042330/http://www.themoscowtimes.com/opinion/article/south-ossetia-looking-much-like-a-failed-state/408685.html |archive-date=8 July 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/oct/08/georgia-russia-border-conflict-south-ossetia |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |first=Piotr |last=Smolar |title=Georgia wary of Russian encroachment |date=8 October 2013 |access-date=16 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170306141601/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2013/oct/08/georgia-russia-border-conflict-south-ossetia |archive-date=6 March 2017 |url-status=live |df=dmy-all}}</ref> Since 2008, the South Ossetian government has expressed its desire to [[Proposed Russian annexation of South Ossetia|join the Russian Federation]]; if this occurs, it would end South Ossetia's proclaimed independence. A referendum on this matter has been raised multiple times in domestic politics, but none has taken place.

