The '''Swedish People's Party of Finland''' ('''SPP'''; {{langx|sv|Svenska folkpartiet i Finland}}, '''SFP'''; {{langx|fi|Suomen ruotsalainen kansanpuolue}}, '''RKP''') is a [[list of political parties in Finland|Finnish political party]] founded in 1906. Its primary aim is to represent the interests of the minority [[Swedish-speaking population of Finland]].<ref name="Arter1999">{{cite book |first=David|last=Arter |title=Scandinavian Politics Today |url=https://archive.org/details/scandinavianpoli0000arte |url-access=registration|year=1999 |publisher=Manchester University Press |isbn=978-0-7190-5133-3 |page=[https://archive.org/details/scandinavianpoli0000arte/page/62 62]}}</ref><ref name="Bondeson2003">{{cite book |first=Ulla|last=Bondeson |title=Nordic Moral Climates: Value Continuities and Discontinuities in Denmark, Finland, Norway, and Sweden |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bq8boZgfvHQC&pg=PA20 |year=2003 |publisher=Transaction Publishers |isbn=978-0-7658-0203-3 |page=20}}</ref><ref name="PrakkeKortmann2004">{{cite book|author=Hans van den Brandhof |chapter=The Republic of Finland|editor1=Lucas Prakke|editor2=Constantijn Kortmann |title=Constitutional Law of 15 EU Member States|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=b5vZMPbnNXcC&pg=PA183 |year=2004 |publisher=Kluwer |isbn=978-90-13-01255-2 |page=183}}</ref> The party is currently a participant of the [[Orpo Cabinet|Government of Petteri Orpo]], holding the posts of [[Minister of Education (Finland)|Minister of Education]] and [[Minister for European Affairs and Ownership Steering]], along with the post of [[Minister for European Affairs, Culture and Sport|Minister of Youth, Sport and Physical Activity]] until 13 June 2025.

An ethnic [[big tent|catch-all]] party,<ref name="Beyme1996">{{cite book|author=K. Beyme|title=Transition to Democracy in Eastern Europe|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gFqCDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA59|year=1996|publisher=Springer|isbn=978-0-230-37433-1|page=59}}</ref> its main election issue since its inception has been the Swedish-speaking Finns' right to their own language while maintaining the position of Swedish as an official language in Finland.<ref name="Nordsieck">{{Cite web|url=http://parties-and-elections.eu/finland.html|title=Finland|last=Nordsieck|first=Wolfram|year=2019|website=Parties and Elections in Europe|access-date=15 April 2019}}</ref> Ideologically, it is [[liberalism|liberal]],<ref name="Bergqvist1999">{{cite book |first=Christina|last=Bergqvist |title=Equal Democracies?: Gender and Politics in the Nordic Countries |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=t8bfrJvsfJ8C&pg=PA319 |year=1999 |publisher=Nordic Council of Ministers |isbn=978-82-00-12799-4 |page=319}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Juvonen|first=Tuula|date=2016-05-01|title=Out and Elected: Political Careers of Openly Gay and Lesbian Politicians in Germany and Finland|journal=Redescriptions: Political Thought, Conceptual History and Feminist Theory|volume=19|issue=1|pages=49–71|doi=10.7227/R.19.1.4|issn=2308-0914|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Bale |first=Tim |title=Riding the populist wave: Europe's mainstream right in crisis |date=2021 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |others=Cristóbal Rovira Kaltwasser |isbn=978-1-009-00686-6 |location=Cambridge, United Kingdom |pages=34 |oclc=1256593260}}</ref> [[Social liberalism|social-liberal]],<ref name="HloušekKopeček2010">{{cite book|author1=Vít Hloušek|author2=Lubomír Kopeček|title=Origin, Ideology and Transformation of Political Parties: East-Central and Western Europe Compared|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=K79sdX-amEgC&pg=PA204|year=2010|publisher=Ashgate Publishing, Ltd.|isbn=978-0-7546-7840-3|page=204}}</ref> [[Centrism|centrist]],<ref>{{cite book |first1=Jan-Erik |last1=Lane |first2=Svante |last2=Ersson |title=The Nordic Countries: Compromise and Corporatism in the Welfare State|chapter=Political Institutions in Europe|editor-link1=Josep Colomer|author-link1=Jan-Erik Lane|editor=Josep M. Colomer|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TZF8AgAAQBAJ&pg=PA260 |year=2008 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-134-07354-2 |page=260}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2015-03-06|title=Finland MPs vote to keep Swedish in schools|url=https://www.thelocal.se/20150306/finland-mps-vote-to-keep-swedish-in-schools/|access-date=2021-12-21|website=The Local Sweden|language=en-US}}</ref> and [[Pro-Europeanism|pro-European]].<ref name="Program">{{Cite web|url=https://sfp.fi/politik/beslut-politiska-program/riksdagsvalsprogram_2019/|title=SFP:s Riksdag ValsProgram 2019|last=RKP|first=SFP|year=2019|website=SFP|access-date=18 April 2019}}</ref> The party has been in government from 1979 to 2015 and again from 2019, with one or two ministerial portfolios in [[Finnish Government|government]], and has collaborated with parties across the [[left–right political spectrum]] in [[Parliament of Finland|parliament]].

The fact that the winning party in Finland has usually needed the support from the party to form a [[coalition government]] has meant that the SPP has been able to affect the [[politics of Finland]] on a larger scale than the party's actual size would suggest. The continued position of Swedish as one of two [[official language]]s in Finland in the face of a steady erosion of the Swedish speakers' share of the Finnish population from 12% at independence compared to 5% today, as well as the preservation of the Swedish-speaking minority's right to engage in [[Swedish-speaking Finns#Culture, literature and folklore|Swedish culture]], are two examples of the party's influence in Finnish politics. The party is a member of the [[Liberal International]], the [[Alliance of Liberals and Democrats for Europe Party|Alliance of Liberals and Democrats for Europe]], and [[Renew Europe]]. The youth organisation of the party is called [[Svensk Ungdom]] (Swedish Youth).

