'''The Walt Disney Company''' ('''TWDC'''), commonly known as '''Disney''', is an American multinational [[mass media]] and [[entertainment industry|entertainment]] [[conglomerate (company)|conglomerate]] headquartered at the [[Walt Disney Studios (Burbank)|Walt Disney Studios]] complex in [[Burbank, California]]. Founded on October 16, 1923, by brothers [[Walt Disney|Walt]] and [[Roy O. Disney|Roy O. Disney]] as the '''Disney Brothers Cartoon Studio''', the company later operated under the names '''Walt Disney Studio''' and '''Walt Disney Productions''' before adopting its current name in 1986. Disney established itself as a leader in [[animation]] with the release of ''[[Steamboat Willie]]'' in 1928. The short film's pioneering use of [[synchronized sound]] helped popularize [[Mickey Mouse]],<ref name="ribuneCinephoneT">{{Cite news |last=Davis |first=Elizabeth |date=June 25, 2019 |title=Historically yours: Mickey Mouse is born |work=[[Jefferson City News Tribune]] |url=https://www.newstribune.com/news/2019/jun/25/Historically-Yours-Mickey-Mouse-is-born/ |url-status=live |access-date=April 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220430183134/https://www.newstribune.com/news/2019/jun/25/Historically-Yours-Mickey-Mouse-is-born/ |archive-date=April 30, 2022}}</ref> who became the company's mascot and corporate icon.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2003-jul-23-fi-mickey23-story.html |title=M-I-C-K-E-Y: He's the Leader of the Brand |first=Richard |last=Verrier |website=Los Angeles Times |date=July 23, 2003 |access-date=July 8, 2025}}</ref>

After achieving success in animation by the early 1940s,<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Books |first=LIFE |date=2016-05-13 |title=How World War II Changed Walt Disney |url=https://time.com/4326360/walt-disney-world-war-ii-excerpt/ |access-date=2025-03-07 |magazine=TIME |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Allison |first=Austin |date=2021-10-14 |title=How Disney Animation's Most Forgotten Era Saved the Studio During WWII |url=https://collider.com/disney-animation-package-films-explained/ |access-date=2025-03-07 |website=Collider |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-09-08 |title=Disney Through the Decades {{!}} Cornell Cinema |url=https://cinema.cornell.edu/news/disney-through-decades |access-date=2025-03-07 |website=cinema.cornell.edu |language=en}}</ref> Disney expanded into live-action films, television and [[theme parks]] during the 1950s. Following Walt Disney's death in 1966, the company experienced a decline in profitability, particularly in its animation business. In 1984, [[Michael Eisner]] became CEO and oversaw a revival of the company through international theme park expansion and the successful [[Disney Renaissance]] era of animation from 1989 to 1999. After becoming CEO in 2005, [[Bob Iger]] continued Disney's expansion into a major entertainment conglomerate through the acquisitions of [[Pixar]] in 2006, [[Marvel Entertainment]] in 2009, [[Lucasfilm]] in 2012 and [[21st Century Fox]] in [[Acquisition of 21st Century Fox by Disney|2019]]. In 2020, [[Bob Chapek]] succeeded Iger as CEO, but was dismissed in 2022, leading to Iger's return.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 20, 2022 |title=Disney Shocker! Bob Iger Back As CEO, Bob Chapek Out |url=https://deadline.com/2022/11/disney-bob-iger-returns-ceo-bob-chapek-out-1235178223/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230815230759/https://deadline.com/2022/11/disney-bob-iger-returns-ceo-bob-chapek-out-1235178223/ |archive-date=August 15, 2023 |access-date=August 15, 2023 |website=Deadline}}</ref> [[Josh D'Amaro]] succeeded Iger as CEO in March 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 18, 2026 |title=Disney Annual Shareholders Meeting: New CEO Josh D'Amaro Speaks to Company's Next Chapter of Creativity, Innovation, and Connection |url=https://thewaltdisneycompany.com/news/2026-annual-shareholders-meeting-remarks/ |website=[[The Walt Disney Company]] |access-date=October 24, 2026}}</ref>

Disney operates one of the largest film and television production businesses in [[Hollywood, Los Angeles|Hollywood]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Koblin |first1=John |last2=Barnes |first2=Brooks |last3=Mullin |first3=Benjamin |last4=Grynbaum |first4=Michael |date=September 18, 2025 |title=Disney Pulled Jimmy Kimmel as Pressure Built on Multiple Fronts |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/18/business/media/disney-abc-jimmy-kimmel.html |access-date=September 22, 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> Its [[Walt Disney Studios (division)|Walt Disney Studios]] division includes [[Walt Disney Pictures]], [[Walt Disney Animation Studios]], [[Pixar]], [[Marvel Studios]], [[Lucasfilm]], [[20th Century Studios]], [[20th Century Animation]] and [[Searchlight Pictures]]. Disney's other businesses encompass the [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC television network]]; cable networks including [[Disney Channel]], [[ESPN]], [[Freeform (TV channel)|Freeform]], [[FX (TV channel)|FX]] and [[National Geographic (American TV channel)|National Geographic]]; publishing, merchandising, music and theatrical productions; [[direct-to-consumer]] streaming services including [[Disney+]], [[Hulu]] and ESPN's [[ESPN (streaming service)|streaming offerings]]; and [[Disney Experiences]], which operates theme parks, resort hotels and [[Disney Cruise Line|cruise lines]] worldwide.

Disney is one of the world's largest and most widely recognized entertainment companies.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/marisadellatto/2023/06/08/the-worlds-largest-media-companies-in-2023-comcast-and-disney-stay-on-top/ |title=The World's Largest Media Companies In 2023: Comcast And Disney Stay On Top |first=Marisa |last=Dellatto |website=Forbes |date=June 23, 2023 |access-date=June 23, 2023}}</ref> The company is widely regarded as an influential force in the entertainment industry, particularly for its contributions to animation and filmmaking. Disney ranked 71st on the 2025 [[Forbes Global 2000|''Forbes'' Global 2000]]<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Schifrin |first1=Andrea |last2=Murphy |first2=Matt |title=Forbes' 2025 Global 2000 List - The World's Largest Companies Ranked |url=https://www.forbes.com/lists/global2000/?sh=51d599675ac0 |access-date=2026-06-18 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref> and 44th on the 2026 [[Fortune 500|''Fortune'' 500]] list of the largest American companies by revenue.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Walt Disney |url=https://fortune.com/company/disney/ |access-date=2026-06-18 |website=Fortune |language=en}}</ref> Since its founding, the company has won 135 [[Academy Awards]], including 26 awarded to Walt Disney himself. Its films have appeared on numerous [[List of films considered the best|lists of the greatest films of all time]], and the company has played a major role in the development of the theme park industry. Publicly traded since 1940, Disney is listed on the [[New York Stock Exchange]] (NYSE) and has been a component of the [[Dow Jones Industrial Average]] since 1991. As of August 2020, approximately two-thirds of its shares were held by large financial institutions. Disney celebrated its [[Centennial|100th anniversary]] on October 16, 2023.

