{{Yemeni Armed Forces sidebar}}

The '''Yemeni Armed Forces''' ({{langx|ar|الْقُوَّاتُ الْمُسَلَّحَةُ الْيَّمَّنِيَّة|al-Qūwāt al-Musallaḥa al-Yamaniyya}}) are the military forces of the Republic of [[Yemen]]. They include the [[Yemen Army#Pro-Hadi/Alimi forces|Yemeni Army]] ([[Yemeni Ground Forces]] and [[Republican Guard (Yemen)|Republican Guard]]), [[Yemeni Navy|Yemeni Naval Forces and Coastal Defense]] (including the Marines) and the [[Yemeni Air Force|Yemeni Air Forces and Air Defense]] (including the Air Defense Force) and the [[Homeland Shield Forces]], for the [[Supreme Political Council|SPC]], it includes the Yemeni Army, Navy, Air Force, and the Strategic Reserve Forces & Unmanned Aviation Arm & the Rocket Force.

Since the start of the [[Yemeni civil war (2014–present)|current civil war]] in 2014, the armed forces have been divided; at first with one side being loyalists of the former president [[Ali Abdullah Saleh]] and [[Houthis|Houthi]] fighters and the other side being pro-Yemeni government forces of former president [[Abdrabbuh Mansour Hadi]]; as of 2026, between the internationally recognized [[Presidential Leadership Council]] (PLC), and the [[Houthi]]-led [[Supreme Political Council]] (SPC). to distinguish between Houthi & Yemeni forces, the internationally-recognized government of Yemen since 2015 uses the '''Yemeni National Army (YNA)''' to describe its army, while the Houthis & Saleh loyalists (2014–2017) kept the '''Yemeni Army''' identity. Per the constitution, the [[President of Yemen]] serves as the commander-in-chief. Currently, the presidency and supreme command of the armed forces is disputed between [[Rashad al-Alimi]], Chairman/President of the PLC, and [[Mahdi al-Mashat]], Chairman/President of the SPC. Before the civil war, the united military (1990–2014/2015) was headquartered in the country's capital, [[Sanaa]]. After the beginning of the Yemeni civil war (2014/2015–present), the pro-Houthi/SPC Yemeni Armed Forces are headquartered in the country's capital, [[Sanaa]], and the Yemeni Armed Forces loyal to the internationally-recognized government of Yemen are headquartered in [[Aden]] between 2015 and 2019 and later since 2026, and were previously headquartered in [[Marib|Ma'arib]] from 2019 to 2026.

Already before 2014, the number of military personnel in Yemen was relatively high; in sum, Yemen had the second largest military force on the [[Arabian Peninsula]] after [[Saudi Arabia]]. In 2012, total active troops were estimated as follows: army, 66,700; navy, 7,000; and air force, 5,000. In September 2007, the government announced the reinstatement of [[compulsory military service]]. Yemen's [[defense budget]], which in 2006 represented approximately 40 percent of the total government budget, is expected to remain high for the near term, as the military draft takes effect and internal security threats continue to escalate.

